/*! \page dapp_observability dApp Metrics and Copy Accounting

Operational enable/disable and monitoring steps live in \ref dapp_quickstart.
This page defines the metric name/unit contract and copy-accounting rules that
those monitors must interpret.

\section dapp_metric_contract Metric contract

Metric names, units and meanings are part of the public dApp contract. The
canonical cross-vendor names are defined in
\c ocudu/dapp/abi/v1/metric_catalog.h. An implementation may add metrics in a
vendor namespace, but must not publish an equivalent value under a different
name or unit.

Every descriptor is either a cumulative counter
(\c OCUDU_DAPP_METRIC_COUNTER_V1) or a point-in-time gauge
(\c OCUDU_DAPP_METRIC_GAUGE_V1). Counters never decrease during one instance
generation. Monitoring systems compute rates from two counter samples and
their monotonic timestamps; the DU does not maintain unbounded time series.
Unknown flags and a descriptor claiming both kinds are rejected. A zero flag
is retained only for ABI-v1 compatibility with early modules.

The standard module metrics include \c dapp.invoke.count,
\c dapp.invoke.latency_ns, \c dapp.fallback.count and
\c dapp.breaker.open. A module publishes only values it can measure honestly;
the host-owned process metrics remain separate from application metrics.

\section dapp_copy_contract Copy contract

"Zero-copy" means that no tensor byte moves after producer readiness for the
selected placement. Metadata construction, descriptor transfer and cache-line
traffic do not turn a tensor snapshot into zero-copy. The canonical reasons
are:

- \c lease: the consumer borrows producer storage under an explicit lifetime;
- \c snapshot: CPU tensor bytes are copied into an isolated process route;
- \c export: tensor bytes are copied into a dedicated accelerator export pool;
- \c e3_payload: tensor bytes are materialized into a portable E3 payload.

Each reason has a cumulative operation count and, where bytes move, a byte
counter. The implemented CPU process route increments exactly one snapshot
operation and the tensor byte length only after the shared-ring record commits.
A full or invalid publication increments a drop counter and no copy counter.
Tensorless coordination records increment publication/payload counters but no
tensor-copy counter. \c class_c.route.payload_bytes includes the complete
record (header, metadata, padding and tensor), while
\c class_c.copy.snapshot.bytes contains tensor bytes only. This distinction
makes a second bulk copy visible instead of hiding it in transport overhead.

Per-route targets retain these fixed atomic counters. The supervised instance
reports their bounded aggregate through the ordinary E3 metric query alongside
process health. No formatting, allocation, ASN.1 work or management lock is
added to a producer callback.

\section dapp_metric_benchmark Benchmark

Build and run the OCUDU-style non-sanitized benchmark with:

\code{.sh}
cmake --build <release-build> --target dapp_runtime_benchmark
<release-build>/tests/benchmarks/dapp/dapp_runtime_benchmark -R 10000
\endcode

It reports P50/P75/P90/P99/P99.9/P99.99/worst for unbound and disabled hook
bypass, active native Class-C dispatch, Class-A CE fixture dispatch and output
validation, scheduler context acquire/release, a four-receive-port full-slot
CPU process snapshot, and authenticated process get-config forwarding. It also
asserts the exact snapshot-copy count and bytes. The banner records host,
machine, kernel, compiler and build kind.

Sanitizer, emulated, assertion-heavy, frequency-unstable or contended runs are
functional evidence only and must never be published as latency qualification.
Production admission additionally requires isolated target-hardware runs,
deadline miss rates, allocation/copy counters, and end-to-end modem impact.

*/
